speechtext:
Perhaps another minister's view of what constitutes 80 per cent Canadian content will be different. I have never been ashamed to admit that Reader's Digest enters my home. I think it is a fine family magazine, fit for children to read. I welcome it;
I welcome its wholesomeness. The magazine does not resort to four-letter words, pornography or near pornography, in order to attract reader attention. In recent years, 30 per cent of its material has been of Canadian content and its articles are attractive to Canadians. It prints factual accounts of Canadian geography, human interest stories about Canadians, and interesting, readable vignettes about our history.
The Secretary of State (Mr. Faulkner) spoke about taking the advice of advisers in his department before proposing Bill C-58 as now printed. He ought to have consulted experts in literary matters. Perhaps he did. Perhaps he overlooked the expertise of the hon. member for Vancouver-Kingsway (Mrs. Holt) who in her interesting and logical speech last night showed that she possesses knowledge and expertise of literary matters.

If I may be permitted, Mr. Speaker, in response to the question raised by the hon. member for Peace River (Mr. Baldwin). The section he quoted in the regulations, section 221, provides that:
The Governor in Council may make regulations (a) prescribing anything that, by this Act, is to be prescribed or is to be determined or regulated by regulation;
Section 19 does not give that authority.
